The primary vulnerability is client-side prototype pollution, which is subsequently chained into DOM-based XSS.

Key Takeaways
Client-side prototype pollution can originate from URL query parameters.
__proto__ should be treated as a dangerous property when processing user-controlled input.
JavaScript automatically searches the prototype chain when resolving properties.
Inherited properties can therefore influence application behavior.
Prototype pollution becomes significantly more dangerous when a suitable gadget exists.
Dynamic assignment to script.src can provide a powerful DOM XSS sink.
Object.hasOwn() can prevent applications from unintentionally trusting inherited properties.
DOM Invader can automate the discovery of prototype pollution sources and gadgets.
Conclusion

The application was vulnerable to DOM XSS via client-side prototype pollution.

An attacker could inject:

__proto__[transport_url]

through the URL query string and modify the global:

Object.prototype

The polluted property was subsequently inherited by the application's config object and accessed through:

config.transport_url

The application then assigned the attacker-controlled value to:

script.src

By supplying:

data:,alert(1);

the attacker was able to execute arbitrary JavaScript in the context of the application.

